+#!/usr/bin/env node
+/*
+ * Mock2 gate runner (1.42.0): runs the gates a release prompt states, one section at a time, and passes
+ * values between commands itself, never through a shell variable (PRO current §P.2, §P.3).
+ *
+ * node .mock2/bin/gates.mjs <prompt.md> --section <n> [--dry-run]   runs section n's gates in order
+ * node .mock2/bin/gates.mjs <prompt.md> --resume <n> [--dry-run]    runs section n out of order
+ * node .mock2/bin/gates.mjs <prompt.md> --check                     parses and validates; runs nothing
+ *   --json            the result as JSON on stdout
+ *
+ * Sections. A heading `## Step <n> — <title>` (or `## <n>. <title>`, levels 2–4, `Section` also read)
+ * opens section n. Gate blocks are fenced code blocks whose info string is `gate`; every other fenced
+ * block is prose. A gate block belongs to the section above it and holds one line per key:
+ *
+ *   done-when: <command>          section-level: exit 0 means the section is already done (exit 3);
+ *                                 a `readonly: true` line straight after it (before any gate:) marks it
+ *                                 read-only, the only kind a --dry-run executes
+ *   gate: <name>                  starts a gate (letters, digits, . _ -; unique in its section)
+ *   run: <command>                the command, run by /bin/sh -c in the current directory
+ *   readonly: true                the gate writes nothing: the only gates a --dry-run executes
+ *   expect: exit <n>              the exit status (default: 0)
+ *   expect: /<regex>/<flags>      stdout, trailing newlines removed, matches the regex
+ *   expect: <exact text>          stdout, trailing newlines removed, equals the text
+ *   note: <text>                  printed with the gate's result
+ *   raw-saved: true               lets this gate's commands use saved values of any characters (below)
+ *   SAVE NAME=<command>           after the gate passes, runs the command and saves its stdout
+ *   SAVE NAME='<literal>'         saves the literal value
+ *
+ * A line ending in ` \` continues on the next line. Several `expect:` lines must all hold; text and
+ * regex expectations also need exit 0 unless an `expect: exit <n>` says otherwise. A gate has a run:, a
+ * SAVE or both.
+ *
+ * Saved values. `${SAVED:NAME}` is the only substitution, and the runner performs it. In a command the
+ * value is never spliced into shell text: the child gets it as the environment variable
+ * MOCK2_SAVED_NAME and the reference becomes "${MOCK2_SAVED_NAME}" (unquoted context, quotes added) or
+ * ${MOCK2_SAVED_NAME} (inside double quotes), so the shell expands it once, with no word splitting,
+ * globbing or command substitution of the value. It may stand unquoted, in double quotes or inside
+ * $( … ); inside single quotes or backticks it is refused. The quoting protects the value only from the
+ * shell the runner starts: a command that hands text on to another shell (sh -c, a pipe into sh, xargs,
+ * eval, ssh, su, docker exec, find -exec) re-parses it, and no list of such spellings is complete. So a
+ * saved value used in a run:, SAVE or done-when: command must hold nothing but letters, digits and
+ * _ . : / @ + = , - (commits, digests, versions, times, paths, modes); any other value is refused (exit
