+- **A consumer is reused without its build.** A build declares an identity, not a cache: a check's
+  `identity` block (inputs, tools, env) gives it an input fingerprint computed without running it. A
+  check never carries both `identity` and `reuse`, so a build is never cached and nothing restores an
+  artifact. A consumer that declares `reuse.buildInputs` may be reused without the artifact when its
+  own inputs and its build's declared identity are unchanged; the build is then recorded `not
+  applicable — no selected consumer needed its artifact`, which is neither a pass nor a missing check.
+  If the consumer executes, the build executes first. So it does when a check that depends on the build
+  through the consumer executes.
+- **A battery carries trusted reuse into its clone.** A passed final battery writes the cache entries
+  of its reuse-eligible checks back into the worktree's cache and names their keys in its report; a
+  failed or interrupted battery writes nothing back. Seeding a clone is off unless the battery is
+  asked to seed from the worktree's cache or the queue record, and only the repository's own cache or
+  queue record is a source. An entry whose recorded fingerprint does not equal the key the clone
+  recomputes is a named miss and the check executes; a miss is never an error. A seeded result keeps
+  its original run and time and is labelled `reused — inputs unchanged`. The inherited-environment
+  fingerprint leaves out the documented runner-owned variables (run, task and hold identity, budget
+  wait, Relay settings and the battery directory), which change per run and decide nothing a check
+  computes. Reuse across batteries needs `battery.cleanEnv: true` in `checks.json`, off by default:
+  the clone then runs with a stated environment, and the report and `doctor` name every variable it
+  dropped, by name only.
+- **A slow file named for repair is repaired.** When the same file is the slowest and takes more than
+  half of its check's test time in three runs running, the runner names it for repair, and the
+  handoff names it as known failures are named. A slow file named for repair is repaired at the test
+  (split per state, shared fixture built once) or recorded as a FOLLOW-UP with its cost per battery; a
+  longer timeout is not a repair.
+- **The runner notices a changed candidate when each heavy check finishes**, not only at the end. On a
+  difference it reports the heavy checks not yet started as `not-run`, naming the time and the first
+  changed path, and lets cheap checks finish; `check.mjs --no-early-stop` keeps the comparison at the
+  end only. Commit, then run: records edits go in the shared checkout (§M.4) or are made once the run
+  ends.
