-  "changelog": "1.40.0: Downloadable offline source ZIP, exact source identity, SHA-256 checksums and transfer instructions for local-folder upgrades, VS Code and Git rebasing. 1.39.0: The application foundation stack. Every new PRO application is provisioned by its host with PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector, S3-compatible object storage (SeaweedFS by default; any S3-compatible provider such as Cloudflare R2 instead) and a local embedding service (development quality, with a warning to switch to OpenAI or another provider), as one Compose unit in its own deployment. The database is reached over a local socket with peer authentication; its only network listener, on loopback, admits just the read-only reporting role, with SCRAM. Storage and embeddings switch off per deployment and no switch deletes data; bundled storage that holds objects stays on until a person picks a replacement and either copies everything, verified, or deliberately starts fresh. An embedding model or dimension change re-embeds into a new validated index; vector spaces are never mixed and failover never crosses them. Feature code reaches objects, embeddings and vector search only through the host's adapters. Secrets are held encrypted by the host, never in env files. Readiness is reported only after a real operation, and mock and local providers say what they are. SQLite and other engines are refused for any server role unless the developer explicitly asked and a person recorded the request on the project; DATABASE_URL is required and a missing database fails start-up, never a fallback. A new application records Profile: cpr; none is for existing repositories. pgvector is required for new applications and the stack's images are pinned. Existing applications are not changed; moving one onto the stack is a migration a person confirms, from a restorable snapshot, with the database verified table by table, rollback on failure or on request, and nothing deleted (PRO current \u00a7W.6; \u00a7D, \u00a7J.4, \u00a7J.9, \u00a7Q, \u00a7R, \u00a7W.3; Addendum A.2, A.8). 1.38.0: Numeric targets are planned before code. A Done-when item that carries a number (latency, size, throughput, memory, time, count) gets a line under Budget estimates in the Authorized scope: the mechanism expected to meet it and the rough arithmetic, rate \u00d7 cost per unit against the budget, with what grows over the longest expected session. An estimate that misses is resolved in the design before code, or marked [OPEN] with the design assumed until the first measurement settles it. With no numeric target nothing is added, so a small change keeps three lines; a small change with a number adds the estimate as a fourth. Each failure case states the rule the design keeps. The measurement runs against the first working skeleton, runs that prove scale, latency and durability come before feature and visual runs, and a result reads measured vs target with PASS or FAIL. Work on a shared component starts from its docs and recorded decisions, and what a cycle learns goes back into them (in scope when the cycle touches those docs, otherwise a follow-up). Review checks the estimates and results; a missing estimate is a low follow-up, never back-filled. Text only; none of it holds up building, testing or committing (capacity \u00a75, \u00a710, \u00a713; PRO current \u00a7E). 1.37.0: Internal modules and guided reuse. Setup infers boundaries and records reuse intent while preserving explicit decisions. Compiler-backed checks report private imports, undeclared dependencies, cycles and unverified coverage. Read-only source-folder plans expose compatibility, host bindings, namespace conflicts, migrations and exact changes; selected application preserves local source and catalog customizations. Real receiving-project interaction evidence is separate from declarations and fake-host checks. Existing applications are never automatically converted or transferred. 1.36.0: Task worktree lifecycle. Durable ownership and receipts in the common Git directory support read-only inventory, bounded closeout plans and interruption recovery. Integration or self-contained preservation, protected-content checks, exact worktree identity and current host inactivity/admission evidence guard selected closeout. Checkout, branch and registration cleanup remain separate; existing backlog is not automatically removed. Host process management and scheduling remain explicit adapter responsibilities. 1.35.0: Portable Git readiness. Read-only host and project observations distinguish an absent tool, an unversioned folder, an empty initialization and a committed baseline. Authorized preparation uses a reviewed bounded selection, preserving existing history and user edits. An optional remote keeps that local history and uses explicit safe synchronization with truthful offline, conflict and uncertain states. Existing first-time setup and build preparation reuse the readiness result; host installation, credentials, scheduling and durable execution remain adapter responsibilities. 1.34.0: Integrated application first-time setup. Existing init and resumable setup collect purpose, audience, access, data, experience and first-version scope; a project-owned blueprint keeps research provenance, assumptions and conditional module choices. A composable base application generator provides the selected foundation with secure instance owner setup and server permissions. Adoption and standards upgrades preserve application source and answers. Generated source, configured dependencies and demonstrated live behavior remain distinct. 1.33.0: Configurable agent routing. A deterministic tool resolves default-on auto mode, provider profiles and per-role model/effort overrides across personal, project and chat scope; direct bypass and off stop new dispatches, with personal off retained until explicitly reenabled. Native adapters plan and apply owned role definitions while preserving existing settings, report unsupported routes and required host actions, and distinguish requested from actual models. Chat requests use the same settings tool. No persistent service or external provider runner is implied. 1.32.1: Names out of the public text. Mock2's public text names concepts and abilities only: the names of other products, tools used as idea sources, applications, customers, people, companies and hosts, and one deployment's paths, job ids and private commits, are replaced by what they stood for (\"one hosted application\", \"the hosting product\", \"the build environment\", \"an AI IDE's requirement phrasing\"). That covers the PRO v1.1 text and its Addendum A, kept as published except that names of other products, applications, customers and people, and the file names and identifiers that pointed to them, are generalised, with their figures kept; PRO current; the run-ledger rule, the define prompt and the template's production checklist; the Relay example, whose comment author is now a role, and its overview image, re-rendered from the example; the public demo, rebuilt, with six old build files removed; the site, the generator's defaults and mock2-core's own records, where the published run ledger's model cells read \"not published\" and three review screenshots blur their small thumbnail of the earlier image. No rule changes meaning, and projects receive only corrected text and images. A repository seeded earlier keeps its own state/production-checklist.md and may delete the old example comment in it. mock2-core gains its own names check, public-names.test.mjs, run in its suite and before every publication: names read with words split at case and digit changes, across line breaks, spaced and joined, and in file paths, against a published hashed list that holds only names an earlier release already published (the private list never leaves the build environment); shape rules for deployment and home paths, private addresses, job ids, emails, model ids, session links and ticket ids; one list of allowed hosts, with or without a scheme; and a list of reviewed images. Nothing new ships to project repositories as a check, and 000-READ-FIRST.md states the rule for authors. Relay stays 1.2.1; only its example data and example image changed. 1.32.0: Storage, retention and cleanup. A new rule for all work, mock2-storage.md, and PRO current \u00a7V say that nothing is removed without a retention setting that is on or a cleanup a person selected, and that storage growth is information, never a gate. A storage check is read-only, runs nothing from the application and reads a repository only as an unprivileged user; it shows an application's storage by category, logical beside allocated size and the space snapshots hold, counting a shared file once and naming what it could not measure. No cleanup removes the running application, data, secrets, records, a shared backing directory or a protected release (the live, the previous and every candidate); nothing is eligible only because it is large or old, and an age that could not be measured makes nothing eligible; and neither a cleanup nor the sealing of a release follows a link, even one swapped in during the run. A release cap counts the live release and the previous one, taken from the deployment history, never a candidate, and removes the oldest releases outside the protected set first (\u00a7K.2). A cleanup is a person's selection (none, one, several, categories or all eligible; nothing preselected; snapshots only by a separate, explicit choice; one approval), rechecked before each deletion, rescanned afterwards and audited. Retention settings per application come with documented defaults (2 releases counting the live and the previous one, 3 snapshots, caches and test artifacts 14 days, temporary files 2 days, rotated logs 14 days and, on the application's own machine, a 256 MB journal) that the host turns on for a new application it creates empty and proposes, with their effect first, for an existing, imported, cloned, restored or migrated one; log retention removes rotated logs only, and a kept database dump keeps the files that restore it. Storage alerts notify and never block; the PRO rule, the production checklist and capacity \u00a77, where an agent removes the clones, runtimes and worktrees it created, carry the short forms. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.31.0: An update by hand on a Linux server, without PowerShell. repo-init.sh --check prints the repository's release, the published one and what an update would add, update, keep and leave unchanged, and changes nothing, so a server may run it on a schedule. repo-init.sh --update downloads the release's own upgrader with every module it imports, checks each against release.json, and lands the release on a new branch mock2/standards-<version> as one commit of exactly the paths it wrote, with installed.json and the executable bits; each Mock2 file the repository adjusted is kept with the release's version beside it as <name>.mock2-new, which is never committed, and nothing is pushed. It never writes through a symbolic link or commits a path that held the developer's own work, makes the branch before it applies, and checks git's identity first. The session-start line that refuses a conflict now ends with that command, doctor lists the .mock2-new files, and the entry block tells every agent to offer the command and how to merge each pair on the update's branch: start from the release's version and re-apply the adjustment. PRO current \u00a7M.9 is amended for it. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.30.0: Background agents. The agent the developer works with, the coordinating agent, may hand independent leaf items of a SPLIT plan that change disjoint files to background agents (a subagent, a background task, a cloud task, a new session), each from a brief in files: the item's context block with Agent:, Owns:, Shared:, Checks: and Returns: lines, and a file the block names when more is needed (a release prompt, the review handoff, or state/briefs/<item id>.md). Each works in its own worktree and branch, commits only the files it owns, starts no background agent of its own, and returns its result as its own section of state/handoff.md, its branch's last commit, which the coordinating agent reads with git show. The coordinating agent alone edits the shared records, integrates in plan order, runs the checks on the integrated tree and cites that run, keeps review in a context that built none of it and leaves promotion to the developer; a background agent that runs a release prompt is its runner and writes that prompt's records, and a surface that cannot start background agents runs the same briefs in turn. Nothing here gates building or testing (.mock2/capacity.md \u00a716, PRO current \u00a7M.11). Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.29.0: A broken release no longer reaches every repository unseen. The standards' publisher smoke-tests each release with mock2-core's release tools, scripts/release-smoke.sh (Linux and macOS) and scripts/release-smoke.ps1 (Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7), which install it from a clean home folder into fresh repositories, against the candidate served locally before it is published and against the live site after. They check the machine install; repo-init.sh in a new repository (on Windows, a repository seeded from the installed template); the path an existing repository takes, a repository seeded with the release before by that release's own repo-init.sh (on Windows, its installed template), given a project commit and then upgraded by its own session-start upgrade (upgrade.mjs --from-site --if-clean --commit); and a commit through the git hooks when the release ships them, which must leave the hooks' own record. Every file is compared with release.json byte for byte, and each check prints PASS or FAIL with counts and the first differing paths. The tools are never shipped to a project repository, and a failure is a checklist item on the release, never a gate. PRO current \u00a7M.9 says a standards release is smoke-tested this way, with a table of the environments tested, supported but not run, or unverified. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.28.0: Checks run on every commit and push without anyone remembering to run them. The template's .githooks/ runs the quick checks (check.mjs --fast) before each commit and the full checks before each push. A hook never blocks and never waits: a check that needs the shared host budget while it is held or busy is reported missing (check.mjs gains MOCK2_BUDGET_WAIT=0), and a run still going after 60 s before a commit or 600 s before a push is stopped and recorded as not finished. Each hook prints at most three lines and records the run with the commit or the push in .mock2/evidence/hooks/, a folder that ignores itself; a pre-commit record says when the work tree held changes the commit leaves out. Skipping is allowed (--no-verify, MOCK2_HOOKS=off), and a commit made with no result is recorded as skipped at the next hook run; nothing runs during a rebase, cherry-pick, merge, revert or bisect, and no hook writes a tracked file. Init and repo-init.sh set core.hooksPath to .githooks, and in an existing repository the session start does (the one after the upgrade that brings the hooks, and the first in a fresh clone), only when Git has no other hooks path and the repository no hooks of its own; otherwise they set nothing and say how to opt in, and core.hooksPath .git/hooks or MOCK2_HOOKS=off keep the hooks off. The template records the hooks executable, and an upgrade with --commit records the bit with core.fileMode=false too, and the session start records it for the release's executable files an earlier commit left without it. The post-commit hook also refreshes the repository map, so refresh.mjs --install-hook adds no second set of hooks, and doctor reports the hooks' state and the last result. Not a gate; Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.27.0: Each release's turns can be counted. The run ledger gains six optional columns after release: nudges (the developer's messages that only kept the task going, each counted once), item, feature, accepted, regressions and weakened; an older ledger stays valid, earlier rows are never back-filled, and a value nothing observed is unknown, never 0, so accepted, regressions and weakened record unknown until later work feeds them. STOPPED SHORT stays the result value and is counted there. The new report tool node .mock2/bin/measures.mjs reads every ledger table by column name and the change records, prints per version the turns, minutes, STOPPED SHORT turns, nudges, tool calls, checks, deploys, accepted, regressions, weakened, runs per feature and change records with the number of unknown rows in each total, and names rows its headers cannot read; --out replaces only a summary it wrote. The counts describe different work per version and are not a speed claim; they carry no target, threshold or per-person view, and the pooled delivery figures stay the host's (PRO current \u00a7I and \u00a7U, amended). 1.26.0: Small changes stay small, and large requests are split before work starts. When no valid plan in state/work.md covers a request, it is sized. With no sizing signal (more than 4 points, a new data model, a new integration, several screens, or another that .mock2/capacity.md \u00a72 names) a change takes the small path: the rule it serves as one [draft] line in state/rules.md with its Given/When/Then example on the same line (or the existing rule it serves; none when behavior is unchanged), a test named after that rule, then the change, under a three-line scope record, with no mockup or interview unless asked; nothing waits for the answer to the rule's read-back. With a signal the request is classified first: FITS starts its one run, SPLIT splits the work into runs before it starts, new or changed screens get Concept and new rules get Define, in the order Concept, Define, Build, Check and Run, and a split is also shown to the user in plain words, one line per run saying what they will be able to see after it. Running /mock2-concept or /mock2-define is a request for that stage. .mock2/capacity.md \u00a72 and \u00a75 carry a worked example of each path, and the entry block, the constitution (\u00a72, \u00a712), the capacity rule and the Concept, Define and Build prompts and agents say the same. repo-init.sh now writes each template file's bytes exactly as published, downloading to a temporary file beside the target and moving it into place: before, the two Relay PNGs lost their NUL bytes and five text files gained or lost a final newline, so an upgrade took them for local edits; with --force a file is now replaced rather than written through (a symbolic link is replaced, and only the executable bit is kept), and a directory where a file goes is reported. 18 new tests (sizing.test.mjs 14, repo-init.test.mjs 4), each failing on 1.25.0; not a gate, and Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.25.0: This release includes the previously unpublished 1.24.0 session-start updater and adds opt-in parallel browser verification. The browser runner admits one group through the existing shared host budget and runs up to three isolated viewport lanes, each with one worker and ordered fixture stages. It verifies the complete planned inventory against actual results, rejects missing or retried cases, keeps mutable homes and temporary files outside sealed artifacts, waits for owned child-process cleanup and retains the serial checks. A consuming application supplies its fixture, port and data adapters before opting in; pulling the framework alone does not safely parallelize an existing suite. 1.24.0: Check first, then pull or start working. A repository brings itself up to the latest published release at the start of a session when it safely can, and otherwise starts working on the version it has. upgrade.mjs --from-site reads manifest.json and release.json from the site (5 s to a first byte, 60 s for the whole run; MOCK2_SITE or a URL names another site); when the release is newer than .mock2/standards/VERSION it downloads the release's template files into a temporary directory, checks each one's sha256 and size against release.json, and plans and applies them exactly as --from does; a file that differs or is missing, or a template manifest naming a file release.json does not list, and nothing is applied. --if-clean applies only when no tracked file has staged or unstaged changes (untracked files do not count, unless the upgrade would write one), no merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert or bisect is in progress, and the plan has no conflict and nothing only --force would replace; a path through a symbolic link is not applied. Otherwise it applies nothing, prints one line saying why (or offline: when the site does not answer) and exits 0. --commit commits exactly the paths the upgrade wrote, with installed.json, as chore(mock2): standards <from> to <to> on the current branch; it runs none of the project's hooks, never pushes, and when git refuses the commit puts every file back. The journal of a run in progress is kept in the git directory, so git add -A never commits it, and a restore after a stopped run keeps a file edited since and takes the files it added out of the index. --quiet prints only the one result line. One command on every surface: Claude Code runs it as node \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.mock2/bin/upgrade.mjs\" --from-site --if-clean --commit --quiet from the template's new .claude/settings.json (project-owned: an upgrade adds it only to a repository without one, and doctor.mjs prints the hook to add to a settings file that lacks it). Copilot's machine SessionStart hook runs it in the session's folder when that is a Mock2 repository, with the machine's upgrader when the repository's own predates 1.24.0, and its hook timeout is now 120 s. Every other agent runs it first, from the repository root, as the entry block says. A box that deploys itself may run it before its checks (.mock2/README.md). PRO current \u00a7M.9 now says what the tool does: a session-start upgrade lands as its own commit on the current branch holding only the upgrade's paths; an upgrade run by hand still goes on its own branch. The upgrader also keeps a file's mode, makes a new executable file executable, reads a CRLF checkout of a release file as that file, and keeps a CRLF entry file's line endings. 19 new tests in upgrade-site.test.mjs and upgrade-site-review.test.mjs, each failing on 1.23.3. Not a gate: with --if-clean the command always exits 0, and nothing blocks a build, a test, a commit or a push. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.3: A damaged TAR archive is incomplete coverage, never a clean pass, learned from one repository whose review found the secret scan passing a truncated archive. secrets.mjs read TAR headers up to the first empty block without checking them, so bytes that are not a TAR, a single empty block, a member that runs past the end of the file, a header with a wrong checksum or stray size digits, missing end records, or a member after the end records were reported as scanned or as no additions. It now requires whole 512-byte blocks, octal size and checksum fields, a checksum that matches (unsigned or signed), each member and its zero padding inside the file, a member name, and two end records followed only by zero blocks; anything else is reported as a damaged archive and makes coverage incomplete. Valid ustar, old-style and gzip archives and the size limits are unchanged. The file is byte for byte the repair that repository carried, so a repository holding it reads as current on upgrade; delete the secrets.mjs.mock2-new an earlier upgrade left beside it. 3 new tests in secrets-coverage.mjs, 2 failing on 1.23.2. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.2: The LOW and NIT findings of the 1.23.0 review, fixed. repo-map.mjs stamps the map with the commit (or no commits, before the first one) and a hash of the index listing (git ls-files -s) and of each tracked file that differs from the index; --check calls the map current when both match, so a map made during uncommitted work stays current until the tree changes again, one made from edits that were then discarded is stale, and before the first commit the index decides. gen.mjs appends inventory entries to the kinds that are arrays when another kind is not, names the kind it skipped and the entries to add by hand, and keeps the file's line endings (CRLF or LF). tokens.mjs --check compares with line endings normalised, so a CRLF checkout of the generated CSS is current. The reviewer agent and the check prompt follow the production checklist: a Done-when rule with no demonstration at all (neither a test nor a recorded exercise by the developer) is IN SCOPE; a rule demonstrated by a test whose name lacks its ID is a LOW FOLLOW-UP. PRO current \u00a7J.9 and the PRO rule name capabilities by their \u00a7J.4 catalog names. The template's test-changed check is no longer fast, so a repository without the check:test:changed script gets no recurring missing warning from --fast; set fast true once the script exists (.mock2/README.md). An existing repository keeps its own checks.json and removes fast there by hand if it wants to. The PRO profile says tokens.mjs writes to the output the developer names and has no default. The entry block's generators paragraph moves to Scope and the refresh hook to orientation. The 1.23.0 records count 29 generator files, state the generated branch's checks inline and cite no build-box paths or job ids. 3 new tests and 3 changed (tools), each failing on 1.23.1; 123 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.1: The six MEDIUM findings of the 1.23.0 review, fixed, each with tests that fail on 1.23.0. gen.mjs never writes through a symbolic link: a target that is one, dangling or not, or whose folder's real path is outside the repository is refused and reported, a file is created only when absent, and a Name with a path separator or .., or a --dir outside the repository, is a usage error. refresh.mjs --install-hook changes an existing hook only when it is a POSIX shell script (sh, bash, dash, zsh, or no #! line); the block goes before the first top-level command that can end the hook (exit, exec), and the report names that line; any other hook, or one the scan cannot follow, is reported as not installed and left as it was. --uninstall-hook removes exactly the bytes the install added, and a block 1.23.0 installed with the blank line before it. The repository map moves to .mock2/derived/repo-map.md, in a folder repo-map.mjs creates with a .gitignore of *, so a repository whose own .gitignore has no line for it stays clean, by hand and from the hook; a map an earlier release wrote at .mock2/repo-map.md is removed when untracked and named when committed, and the template .gitignore drops its line. tokens.mjs and refresh.mjs write CSS only to an output the developer names ($extensions.mock2.out or --out); the template's state/design/tokens.json names none, and an unedited copy of the 1.23.0 file counts the same, so the file an upgrade adds is inert and --check says no output chosen (PRO current \u00a7K.8 stays the developer's decision). The generator stops, writing no entry point, when the entry block holds a mock2 marker it does not expand. The design workflow (\u00a74) and the verification rule name the states catalog: where an app has one, its states are reviewed at /__states and its screenshots feed the visual-regression report (PRO current \u00a7K.6); optional. 8 new tests (tools 7, scoped rules 1), each failing on 1.23.0; 120 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.23.0: Adapted concepts: twenty-three practices from other specification, planning and build methods, each in the form that fits one agent working in sequence for a developer who speaks plain language, with nothing that blocks a build or a test. A rule in state/rules.md is stored as an EARS sentence (When, While, Where, If \u2026 then, or The system shall) with one Given/When/Then worked example, and the test that demonstrates it carries the rule's id and the example in its name; the expert answers in plain language and confirms the rule read back in plain words, and there are no BDD feature files, step definitions or runner (Define prompt, Concept agent, rules template, constitution \u00a72). An unclear point is marked where it sits, [OPEN: <question> \u2014 assuming <assumption>], work continues on the assumption, and only consequential ones become the one to three questions (capacity \u00a713). The Authorized scope record gains Known risks (rabbit holes and no-gos, drafted by the agent, confirmed by the developer) and Appetite (the developer's time, or not set); at closeout the cycle's run-ledger minutes are set beside the appetite, time over it is stated with both numbers and the developer decides; the agent may propose cuts and never makes one, and there is no circuit breaker (capacity \u00a713, the change-record rule, the reviewer agent and check prompt, constitution \u00a712). Each planned leaf item in state/work.md carries a context block (rule ids, files, decisions already made, the worked example) that later turns read instead of re-deriving (capacity \u00a74, work template). The page brief carries a breadboard (places, controls, connections) before the first render, and the mockup and state/inventory.json derive from it (design workflow \u00a73, Concept prompt, constitution \u00a72). Commit messages are typed (feat, fix, docs, test, refactor, chore, perf, build; ! for breaking) and the change record and release notes are drafted from them; nothing enforces it (change-record rule, build prompt, constitution \u00a74). Path-scoped rules: the TypeScript, phones and installed web apps, design workflow, Relay and stack/AI data rules carry a paths: header and are generated into each surface's form (Claude Code .claude/rules/mock2-*.md, Copilot .github/instructions/mock2-*.instructions.md) and into .mock2/standards/rules/SCOPES.md and the entry block's index for every other agent; capacity, change record, production checklist, run ledger, security, verification, integration and PRO apply to all work and load always. Security now loads for all work rather than only src/**. PRO current: an application lists the Base Platform capabilities it uses in app.manifest.json (name, reason, the fake for development and tests) and the host provisions from the declaration, never from inference; a use without an entry is a checklist finding (\u00a7J.9, new). Flags are an optional catalog entry with an OpenFeature-shaped interface (boolean, string and number evaluation with a default given at the call and a context, never throwing), an in-memory fake, and an owner and a removal follow-up in state/work.md per flag; added only when the developer asks, and a production value is a release decision, since commit is not release (\u00a7J.4, \u00a7J.10, new). One schema to the UI's types: the Drizzle schema gives the Zod schemas in contracts/, which the Express routes parse with and the client imports as types; nothing hand-copied, tsc in the check command reports drift (\u00a7K.7, the TypeScript rule's Express runtime section, the profile's layout). Ledger rows gain an optional last release column; a ledger without it stays valid and no id is back-filled (\u00a7I, the run-ledger rule and template). Lead time, change failure rate and time to restore are defined from releases, rollbacks and those rows, pooled, with no target, no threshold and nothing per person (\u00a7U, new). Agents read the documentation for the version the lockfile pins and name it in the change record's Docs consulted line; a scheduled host task reports outdated dependencies as small and major and proposes an S-sized follow-up the developer authorizes, with no bot pull requests, no automerge, and host forks left to their divergence record (\u00a7Q). A DTCG token file with the prefix as an output setting makes \u00a7K.8 option (b) cheap; the decision stays OPEN and option a applies. New production-checklist items for each. Derived files and generators, deterministic, Node built-ins only, nothing to install. bin/repo-map.mjs writes .mock2/repo-map.md (local, ignored): the commit hash and date, tracked files by top directory with line counts, exported symbols of JavaScript and TypeScript files, Express routes and Drizzle tables found by pattern (said to be approximate) and the state/ files, each section capped with what it left out; --check says whether the map is current (made at HEAD, no tracked change since); the entry block's orientation reads it first and refreshes it when stale. bin/trace.mjs reports the rule ids in state/rules.md that no test carries in its name or path, the inventory states the states catalog does not render, and the work items linked to no Done-when id: a short table and one JSON summary line, --json for the whole report. bin/gen.mjs runs the generators in .mock2/generators/ (list, <generator> <Name>, --dry-run, --dir), and the entry block points a standard feature shape at it: list-detail, crud and protected-route write one feature's PRO shape (a Drizzle table, its contract in contracts/, a store and its fake, one router with Zod at the boundary, a request test whose name carries a placeholder rule id, React views with data-* selectors and the feature's catalog states; protected-route answers 401 and 403 through one permission resolver), and states-catalog writes a /__states page mounted only when import.meta.env.DEV, a Playwright spec that screenshots each state and state/states-catalog.json; it never overwrites a file, appends only new entries to state/inventory.json without changing the rest, and what it writes is the project's. The generated contracts import drizzle-zod, now an optional system in stack.versions.json at 0.8.3 with its compatibility evidence (Stack-Versions.md). bin/tokens.mjs writes CSS custom properties from state/design/tokens.json (W3C DTCG: groups, $type, {alias} references, cycles and unknown references reported) with the prefix, output file and themes as settings; a dark theme applies under data-theme and prefers-color-scheme; a CSS file without the generated header is never overwritten; an output path outside the repository (absolute, with a .. segment or through a symbolic link) and a value containing a line break, ;, {, }, < or */ are refused, named and nothing is written; the template's file keeps --t- and data-theme, so \u00a7K.8 stays open with option a in force. bin/refresh.mjs runs the refreshers (tokens, catalog index, map) by hand; --install-hook adds a marked post-commit block that refreshes only the ignored map, never a tracked file, does nothing during a rebase, cherry-pick, merge, revert or bisect and always exits 0, --uninstall-hook removes only that block; opt-in. checks.json: repo-map, trace and tokens are optional report checks (resource none, fast; they exit 0 whatever they find), and test-changed (manual, fast, optional) runs the project's check:test:changed script over MOCK2_CHANGED_FILES, reported missing until the script exists, while test stays the full suite on the production checklist; the README shows a cross-platform Vitest related script. check.mjs unchanged. By upgrade an existing repository receives the managed entry block and the framework files: capacity.md, the standards and rules, the generated scoped-rule files, the five new tools and their shared helper, .mock2/generators/ and the README; state/design/tokens.json, which it does not have, is added and changes nothing until tokens.mjs runs. checks.json, .gitignore and the state templates are project-owned and do not change in an existing repository: one that wants the four new checks, the /.mock2/repo-map.md ignore line, the ledger's release column or the new rules, work and brief template text adds them by hand. Host follow-ups, with no Mock2 tool in this release: provisioning from app.manifest.json, a flag service where a host has none, the scheduled dependency task and its report, and computing and showing the delivery figures. Not added: multi-agent personas, six-week cycles and a betting table, a circuit breaker, a commit-message linter, required release-note files and automerge, agent-invoking hooks and a BDD runner. 29 new tests (tools 25, scoped rules 4), each failing on 1.22.0; 112 in all. Not a gate. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.22.0: Speed and the browser harness, from one host's measurements of where a release's time goes, anonymized. A deploy and a battery no longer collide: a release prompt's step 0 checks for a running battery first, re-checks every 60 seconds for a bounded time, stops without deploying if it stays busy, and re-checks the main branch and production after any wait; a push-only release skips the wait (PRO current \u00a7P.7, new). The verification rule's browser harness retries a page navigation once when the network changed (net::ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED and its equivalents), records the retry with the state's name, and never retries an assertion or anything else; a second network change fails the state. The template ships no general browser-harness helper, so none changes (the Relay package's own parity script is unchanged). bin/check.mjs: the slowest files and the slow-file warnings read Playwright too (its list reporter's per-test times summed by file, its \"Slow test file\" lines, and a JSON report a check names in \"results\", at a path git ignores; for Playwright a file's share is of the suite's test time); --record prints the wall time, the critical path (the check that finished last), when each check finished and the slowest files. bin/doctor.mjs lists the speed settings available but off (no fast check, order config, no known-failures file, an audit not required on a lockfile change, no reuse), each as information with the setting that turns it on, and failFast as a choice; it says whether a second Mock2 block outside the markers is one an earlier template shipped. bin/upgrade.mjs: --remove-legacy-block removes such a block only when it is byte for byte one an earlier template shipped (the template manifest's legacyBlocks, which the generator recovers from the template's history), keeping the file as it was beside it; anything else is reported and left alone. --project-name fills <Project name>, and a terminal is asked for it. Standards-Benchmark.md: comparing a configuration change on the real battery (same candidate and settings, saved reports with matching settings, medians with their range; one run is a smoke test). Tests for each, failing on 1.21.0. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.21.0: The standard's name. CPR, Continuous Production Readiness, is now PRO, Production Readiness Optimization, in current text: the rules, the current baseline, the constitution, the application profile, the entry block and the entry points generated from it, the templates, the tools' messages and the manifest's titles. Citations read \"PRO current \u00a7E\". Section numbers are unchanged, so an older record's \"CPR current \u00a7E\" still finds its text. Only the name changed: the file names (CPR-v1.1-current.md, mock2-cpr.md, profile-cpr.md), the site's /core/cpr/ paths, the stored profile value cpr and the Relay package name @cpr/relay stay as they are, and the readers do not yet accept a pro profile value; a later release is to move those identifiers, readers first. The published CPR v1.1 text, its Addendum A, released versions and earlier changelog entries keep the old name for provenance, and the current baseline's header says that PRO is the standard formerly called CPR; the constitution and the PRO rule say \"formerly CPR\" where they name it. A test keeps the old name out of current text. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.20.0: Verification tooling and the rest of one host's week of review findings, anonymized. bin/check.mjs: known failures are machine-readable (.mock2/known-failures.json: check, failed count, output patterns, tracked inputs and the commit they were proven on), so a failing check every entry covers by exact figures, with its inputs unchanged since the proof, is labelled known baseline and the run's verdict is known-baselines-only (the exit status stays 1: a known failure is never a pass); one that has stood seven days or failed three runs running is named for repair. The report counts tests that never ran and test files that failed, keeps the five slowest test files and a Mock2 tool's scanned scope, and warns when one file takes most of a check or doubles. --fast runs the checks marked fast (the pre-review profile), and every check gets MOCK2_CHANGED_FILES, the files changed against the comparison base with untracked files included. scheduler.failFast (off unless set) starts no further heavy check after a required check fails or is missing for a reason no entry covers; scheduler.order history (opted into; config, the file's order, is the default) starts the checks that took longest when they last ran first, and a battery's clone gets the worktree's recent run reports for it. requiredWhenChanged makes an optional check (the dependency audit) required when its paths change, and an optional check missing three runs running is named. bin/secrets.mjs: project-declared credential shapes in .mock2/secrets.json, and text inside zip and tar archives (outgoing commits, staged, modified and untracked files) scanned as archive!/member; an archive over 64 MiB, damaged, or past a decompression limit (64 MiB an archive, 1 MiB a member) is incomplete coverage, and a Git LFS pointer or an archive the project lists in archives.exclude is reported as excluded. bin/doctor.mjs names a second, unmanaged Mock2 block outside the managed markers and a template placeholder never filled in; install.sh records ~/.mock2/installed.json; once a record exists (an unreadable one counts) it keeps a file edited since the recorded install or not from it, and removes a retired file only when it still matches its recorded hash (an installation with no record yet is replaced as before, where install.ps1 keeps it). The template checks.json marks lint, types and secrets fast, requires the audit when a lockfile changes and orders by history. Tests for each, failing on 1.19.0. Rules and CPR current: the queue's depth, stacked bases, dry runs and reorders (\u00a7M.10); release step 0 generated and dry-run (\u00a7P.6, new); the final battery after review, pre-final batteries labelled, a test-only addition covered without a new battery, data panels with loading, failed and empty states and a ratchet seeded at 0, reuse split by input domain (verification); a builder self-check before the first review and bounded state records (capacity, build agent); one ledger row per coordinator shift (run ledger); route inventories, short records for small changes and the commits that landed (change records, security); a re-review that names the fix behind a finding (reviewer); focus return tested with delays and an unmounted trigger (design workflow); view as within data scope, bomb fixtures for parsers, compliance mode written explicitly (security, init prompt); the audit required on a lockfile change (production checklist). The capacity file's version line points at .mock2/standards/VERSION. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.19.0: Small fixes learned from one host's week of releases, and identifiers out of the public text. bin/battery.mjs: a clone that links the shared event ledger marks the candidate's tracked ledger files skip-worktree, so git and the tools that list files through it no longer see them as deleted (one tool overflowed a 1 MiB buffer on a ledger of over ten thousand files); the secret scan's comparison base is only a ref under refs/remotes/origin/, so a hand-made origin/HEAD naming a local branch is ignored; a battery that sets the run id finishes a run the runner started (run.finished: passed, failed, or cancelled when interrupted), so its runs no longer stay running; a runner that stopped before starting the run, an inherited MOCK2_RUN_ID and MOCK2_RELAY=off get no finish from it. Tests for each, failing on 1.18.0. Verification rule: every suite a test check wraps runs and is reported, and a test file that fails to load counts every test in it as failed; an intended visual change re-records and inspects its baselines, and a no-sideways-scroll check covers inner scroll regions; compare a candidate's battery with the base's under the same settings, and repair a slow test at the test (one in-process database per file, one real-process scenario per test), never with a longer timeout. Security rule: secrets never go on a command line. CPR summary rule: stored documents stay readable across versions; refreshed tables are upserted, never deleted and refilled. TypeScript rule: schedules catch up; refreshed tables; known traps (extension-loading test engines, production column types in test schemas, a stable auth mock, display utilities over hidden, positioned live regions, client departure by close and writableFinished, page styles over a shared primitive, FOR NO KEY UPDATE on referenced rows, one clock, decoders that never trust a declared size). Capacity: closeout is a listed item. Mobile web: a service-worker update reloads only a page that had a controller; one stacking order above the fixed bottom bar. CPR current \u00a7E amended and \u00a7N.13 added; project names and ids removed from \u00a7O, the mobile-web and CPR summary rules, Relay.md, earlier changelog entries and the site's records, and a test keeps work-item, rule and decision ids out of the published text, the manifest and the records. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.18.0: New work starts from the release queue's tip, learned from one host where seven candidates were rebased after their final battery in three days because another release landed first. The final battery is a candidate's final-integration run under CPR current \u00a7E; the release queue is the ordered list of candidates whose final battery passed and that have not reached the main branch, each placed by its first pass and appended by the session whose battery passed. A new build starts its branch from the queue's tip, or from the main branch when the queue is empty or none is kept, and an investigation reads that tip and records it; a build does not rebase while it is built and restacks onto the tip ahead of it just before its final battery; queue order is release order. After the final battery a candidate changes without a new battery only by a move its release runner absorbs at its first step (the candidates ahead of it landing, records-only commits): the runner replays only the candidate's own commits, proves the tree differs from the tested commit only in records files, re-runs the outgoing secret scan and any test that reads the records, pushes the result to the candidate branch and records it as the re-pin. Releasing out of queue order is the developer's decision and every queued candidate not containing the released one restacks; a candidate whose parent changed or was withdrawn restacks and revalidates; review compares with the parent's commit. CPR current \u00a7M.7, \u00a7N.8 and \u00a7P.1 amended and \u00a7M.10 added; the verification rule's default order, the CPR summary, the repository entry block, and the build prompt and agent carry the rule. No runner, script or package change; Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.17.0: Checks that take turns, failures proven honestly, and releases guarded by a list, learned from one host where five builds ran full batteries at once. The host decides one check budget for every tree on it (MOCK2_BUDGET_DIR / MOCK2_SHARED_SLOTS or ~/.mock2/budget.json, with an absolute directory, override a repository's budgetDir and sharedSlots); checks marked resource none start as soon as their dependencies pass and never count against maxParallel; reports keep localQueueMs apart from sharedQueueMs and keep pass/fail counts for test checks; a reservation from an earlier boot is freed and a group-writable budget directory is named with its fix; check.mjs --hold [--for <minutes>] pauses other sessions' new heavy checks for a measurement window (their wait does not count against the queue limit; running checks finish first; a hold ends when stopped or when its time runs out); SIGHUP ends a run like SIGINT; a final verification that could not run says so instead of blaming changed inputs; the runner's history identity covers only the git settings that decide history and upstreams, so an editor writing its own branch hints no longer voids a secret scan; --record reads reports kept in subfolders. New bin/battery.mjs runs the battery in a detached scratch clone with copied dependencies checked by the project's battery.verify commands (npm ls --depth=0 checks package.json ranges, not lockfile pins), scans secrets against the worktree's own remote-tracking refs (its origin/HEAD, else its branch's upstream) rather than the clone's copy of local branches, keeps the report and a local output log under .mock2/evidence/<commit>/ and removes the clone on success, failure or signal; a state/relay linked to the shared Relay ledger is telemetry, never a dirty candidate; copy and placeholder paths must resolve inside the clone. doctor shows the check budget in force, names a malformed host budget, and names a secret scan with no comparison branch and its two fixes (the scanner still never guesses a branch). Text: load-dependent failures proven by exact figures, known failures that mask assertions, honest labels for carried-forward results, browser-harness checks (fail fast on unanswered requests, fonts loaded, constant origin across worktree ports), capped test workers, and test traps (test-cost password hashes, fake servers without idle timeouts, signals not sleeps, engines built once per file) (CPR current \u00a7E, \u00a7M.5, \u00a7M.6, verification and TypeScript rules); a fix answering only LOW findings gets a narrow re-review (\u00a7F); the data disk's flush latency measured in the load test, storage moves verified, shared check slots sized from the load test (\u00a7L); host changes reach the main branch before a promotion (\u00a7N.12); records commits checked against an allowed list, never a count, and scanned before the push (\u00a7P.4). The template checks.json marks the secret scan and audit resource none. Version 1.16.0 is reserved for another release and is not used here. 1.15.0: Evidence that is kept, visible and scoped, learned from one application's upgrade-and-release cycle. Known baseline failures are proven once and kept in state/known-failures.md with the inputs they depend on; a change whose run shows the recorded signature, with none of those inputs changed since the proof, cites it instead of re-running the base. The check runner states how it schedules, which checks may reuse evidence and whether Relay check evidence is on; says why when it cannot identify the candidate; names any link leaving the repository, ignored or not, and reuses nothing while one exists; prints a saved run as a Markdown table with --record; and writes Relay's schema-2 check.recorded only when checks.json opts in with relay.checkEvidence (off in the template and in every existing repository), so installing standards no longer makes a repository emit events an older host refuses; MOCK2_RELAY=off makes the check runner record nothing. The secret scan adds --committed for what a push delivers and says how to scope a scan that has no comparison branch. doctor reports runner settings, candidate identity, uncommitted managed files and unignored runtime state; upgrade asks for the upgrade to land as its own commit. Rules and CPR current \u00a7E, \u00a7H, \u00a7I, \u00a7M.9 and \u00a7S amended: proportionate records, interrupted turns keep their ledger row, producers follow their host's Relay reader. The Relay package is unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.14.0: universal integration foundation with separate new-app and incremental existing-app implementation prompts; scoped identity, versioned outcome contracts, per-connection capability evidence, authorized dispatch, preserved working connections and reusable migration/conformance records. Standards upgrades do not silently migrate applications. 1.12.0: Scope-aware verification and explicit evidence reuse, prerequisite scheduling, shared resource budgets, reusable run evidence, outgoing Git history secret coverage, and paired before/after benchmark guidance. Relay 1.2.0 adds versioned Git history observations, bounded resumable setup lookback, execution/reuse/queue telemetry, blue imported activity, and a contained top-tabs/left-work/right-content layout preserving the complete interface. Readers must upgrade before new event types are emitted. 1.11.3: Framework-neutral Relay integration guidance learned from a verified host installation: preserve the full interface and event protocol while reusing host capabilities, distinguish viewer/producer/control readiness, require real authorization and private evidence, keep examples isolated and initial navigation stable, and verify deployment/source recovery with host-specific evidence. Relay runtime remains immutable 1.1.2. 1.11.2: Relay 1.1.2 no longer focuses its heading on mount, preventing the embedded demo from scrolling the homepage to Relay. User-driven view changes retain accessible heading focus with preventScroll. 1.11.1: Relay 1.1.1 fixes execution replay evidence: attachments appear only after their recorded artifact event at the selected history time; current evidence and exact failed-attempt capture remain available. 1.11.0: Restore Relay 1.1.0 design and feature parity: compact metric filters, labeled activity history, selected-task context, visual connections and raster mockups, structured reports, secondary idea-to-outcome history, improved execution evidence, append-only task metadata and authenticated previews. Add complete host-app integration prompt and visual acceptance matrix; preserve binary feature assets through installation and upgrades. 1.10.0: Relay reusable work-visibility package, append-only events, real check instrumentation, authenticated API and resumable SSE, task execution replay, secondary idea lineage, references and acknowledged requests. Host integration remains explicit. 1.9.0: operating lessons from the week of 2026-09-25 to 10-01, when one repository was worked by up to three build sessions and a release runner at once (38 conversations on two surfaces). CPR-v1.1-current.md \u00a7M.4\u2013\u00a7M.8 (where sessions may run concurrently): each conversation owns a worktree and branch for code, mockups, design records and baselines; where the shared records live in the checkout releases run from, that checkout holds only them, edited by targeted insert after a fresh read and committed by the release step, replacing \u00a7M.1's per-worktree handoff and merged-back state; a script that appends never shrinks a shared file; diverged records are reconciled by union; IDs, package versions and harness ports are reserved across trees; a worktree installs to its own lockfile; pause stops and reports, with the row and handoff written on resume; releases go one at a time; a tool with one session slot is held by one session. \u00a7N.7\u2013\u00a7N.11: where a candidate is deployed before promotion, it is deployed before it is merged; the checkout a deploy reads is part of the release, with step-0 checks and verified effects; a runner that hits a guard stops cleanly; a runner never edits application records through a candidate, and scheduled jobs ship a manual trigger; the deploy's migration guard runs as a test. \u00a7N.4: pack once, after review converges. New section P, release runners and the prompts they run: pin and re-pin, define failure narrowly, copy the repository's own commands, write records by explicit path, supersede conditionally. \u00a7E: what a passing check does not show (pre-fix failure of a regression test, attribution against the base by exact figures, re-checks after a rebase, shared styles, late-loading gates and fresh browsers, authorization-migration replay). \u00a7F: every material fix is re-reviewed, and release prompts are reviewed with the code. \u00a7I: exactly one row per turn, completeness tied to a closeout the task includes, host changes in the row with their procedure. The constitution: \u00a70 says a permission denial is not routed around; \u00a74 separates operator-managed integration credentials from application secrets; \u00a77 gives the reviewer any release prompt; \u00a711 gains the confirmed working habits (limiting words, questions answered rather than built, hearing before a merge or deploy when asked, self-contained questions, check before asserting, estimates and progress notes, stop and save, sectioned releases, retention default, live copy and background processes). The design workflow gains reference precedence, two \u00a73 rows (migrating an existing page; capability first, redesign later), redesign depth, the developer's investigate-mockup-stop instruction for area and screen changes, enforced control sizes in mockups, tokens and text going forward, and the interface patterns confirmed in review as shared defaults. The security baseline adds operator-managed credentials (admin interface, encrypted, never .env), credential binding and rotation, published-port exposure, assistant scope, permission migration, host exchange files and typing of copied rows. The TypeScript rule lists known test traps; the run-ledger rule adds completeness, host changes, one row per turn, estimation from history and a daily report format; the CPR rule, production checklist, capacity \u00a77 and \u00a710, the entry block, the review-handoff template and the reviewer carry the new items. The source application's names were removed from the current text (CPR current \u00a7J.2, the \u00a7K status line) and from the 1.0.0 and 1.1.0 entries; the historical CPR documents are unchanged. 1.8.1: phone viewport-sized shells recalculate height on reload/pageshow and visible resume, as well as load, resize, orientation, fullscreen and display-mode changes. The mobile rule supplies a measured-height watcher with validated VisualViewport/window readings, pinch-zoom handling, bounded settling retries and cleanup; shared bottom insets subtract space the measured shell already excludes, clamped to zero, with design padding additive. CPR current \u00a7O.2\u2013O.5 records lifecycle evidence, fallback tradeoffs, height ownership and single accounting of excluded space. The constitution's device principle, managed entry block, design workflow and production checklist carry the lifecycle requirement. Generated repository standards, entry points, template identities and release hashes are rebuilt from canonical sources. One application's fix is the source example; the owner reported success on 2026-10-01, but repeated cold-start/reload/resume steps and other devices are not inferred from that report. Reference fallback values and retry timings remain project decisions supported by evidence. 1.8.0: phones and installed apps become a design principle, so a machine or repository that pulls the standards with the install or seed script carries it in the always-on constitution, not only in a rule loaded for matching files. The constitution gains \u00a713, design principles: every interface is designed within them from its first mockup, beside accessibility at every level of the design workflow's precedence; they never gate building or testing, and a developer's explicit departure from one is followed and recorded. The first is designed for the device and the mode it is used in: a screen used on a phone is designed for its edges and an app with a web app manifest for the installed view as well as the tab, bottom-anchored controls clear the system navigation bar in both whether or not the phone reports an inset, mockups and checks cover both, and a device check includes a cold start; the pattern and the Android fallback stay in rules/mock2-mobile-web.md. \u00a72's Concept stage names the design workflow and \u00a713, and \u00a70 and \u00a710 now write their CPR citations as CPR \u00a7. The entry block's UI paragraph, the design workflow's precedence and page brief, and rules/mock2-mobile-web.md point to \u00a713; the repo template's state/design/brief.md gains a Design principles section that points to \u00a713 (the brief is project-owned; existing repositories may add it, and \u00a713 and the entry block already carry the principle). Existing repositories get the constitution and the entry block through the usual upgrade. 1.7.1: the fallback for Android, corrected the same day (a recorded decision superseding an earlier one; OPEN until a device observation that includes a cold start). 1.7.0 applied the 48 px only when the phone reported an inset under 1 px and less than 48 px of the screen height lay outside the page; the owner confirmed it, and half an hour later, on a later launch, the installed app drew its bottom bar under the navigation buttons again: one of the measured conditions did not hold on that launch (which one was not measured). rules/mock2-mobile-web.md step 4 (Android that reports too little): --nav-fallback is 48 px whenever the app runs installed (display-mode standalone or fullscreen), the user agent is Android and the phone is in portrait, otherwise 0, with no condition on a measured size beyond the orientation and no probe, re-measured on resize and on a standalone or fullscreen display-mode change; the max() at :root still lets a larger reported inset win; the price, a gap of up to 48 px where Android already keeps the installed page above its bar (possibly the usual case) or gesture navigation needs less, is stated in the decision with the revisit trigger (a device still clips, the platform reports the inset, or the developer rejects the gap) and preferred to a control under the system bar; navFallback takes {standalone, android, portrait}. Verify gains an upper bound on every Android case, so an inset added to the fallback fails, a small reported inset, and two screen.height stubs that guard that the removed condition stays removed (the second, the page already above the bar, keeps 48 px as the decided price); the device check includes a cold start, the app closed fully and opened again more than once. CPR-v1.1-current.md \u00a7O.2: the device check covers a cold start; \u00a7O.3: a device heuristic keeps the fewest conditions that hold on every launch, names its price in the decision, and becomes ACTIVE on a device observation that includes a cold start; the CPR rule and the production checklist's Frontend item carry the cold start and the price. Not a gate. A repository that adopted the 1.7.0 fallback drops its reported-inset and screen-height conditions and the probe. 1.7.0: phones and installed web apps, from one defect (one application, 2026-09-30): an installed app on an Android phone with three-button navigation drew its bottom bar under the navigation buttons while every browser check at 375 px, all rendered in the tab, was green; the fix was confirmed on that phone by its owner. New rule rules/mock2-mobile-web.md, installed to ~/.claude/rules and shipped in .mock2/standards/rules/: an installed app can be drawn edge-to-edge behind the system navigation; viewport-fit=cover goes in only together with the inset padding; one inset variable at :root, max(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px), env(safe-area-max-inset-bottom, 0px)), with every env() given its fallback because an unknown name without one voids the declaration, widened by a script-set --nav-fallback; bars pad and floating elements move; the fallback for Android that reports 0 (installed, Android, portrait, reported inset under 1 px, screen.height - innerHeight < 48, then 48 px), read through a hidden probe, kept in one pure unit-tested function and recorded as a decision; the installed view verified in Playwright with a display-mode stub, an Android user agent, a screen.height override and Chromium's Emulation.setSafeAreaInsetsOverride, asserting clearance with lower and upper bounds; display-mode CSS recorded as not verified; a fail-first build that really is the old code. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section O, phones and installed apps: O.1 an installable application is verified in the mode it is used in; O.2 emulation is a substitute measurement and the device check is recorded as done or not verified; O.3 a device heuristic is an OPEN decision until a device observation confirms it. The entry block's UI paragraph loads the rule for phone screens and for any app with a web app manifest; the design workflow inspects bottom-anchored UI with an inset and the installed view; the production checklist's Frontend group and the CPR rule carry \u00a7O. Not a gate. Existing repositories get the rule and the block through the usual upgrade; an app with no bottom-anchored control needs no script. 1.6.0: operating lessons from the week of 2026-09-19, when one repository was worked by four agent surfaces in one working tree. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section M, several agents and surfaces in one repository. M.1: one git worktree per concurrent session, with its costs named (untracked .env, compose names and ports, decision IDs renumbered before merge); where a tree is shared anyway, new files added by name, commits by explicit path with git commit --only -- <paths> and confirmed with git show --stat HEAD, a file both sessions edited committed only from staged hunks or left uncommitted, and the handoff kept one section per session under a stable label. A shared index had put another session's 41 staged files into an unrelated commit. M.2: each surface's capabilities recorded once under ## Surfaces in .github/copilot-instructions.md, and a new turn result HANDED OFF for a turn that finished everything its surface can do and leaves the rest to a named surface by design, citing that line, with a Finish on another surface section in the handoff. BLOCKED stays for unplanned impossibility; 11 of 12 turns on one surface had ended BLOCKED by design, hiding the real blocks. M.3: a check a surface cannot run is routed to one that can, and a measurement on a substitute engine or version says so. Section N, release and data safety from operation. N.1: a migration record states its lock footprint on existing tables; long write locks on tables the application writes are restructured, with constraints validated in a later transaction than the one that adds them. N.2: the release record lists what a candidate can write to shared data, and scheduled writers run only from the promoted release. N.3: a candidate gateway substitutes its own page only when the upstream is unreachable, never for an application error. N.4: a packed package version is never re-packed. N.5: real-provider verification writes go through the audited path, with a read-back and the restore step stated. N.6: supersession names the superseded decision's ID and updates its status in the same edit; an unstated conflict becomes an OPEN decision; a resource-sizing decision names a resize as a revisit trigger. \u00a7E: secrets is a required check. The repository template ships .mock2/bin/secrets.mjs, a scanner that needs nothing installed: it scans lines not yet pushed (against the merge base with the upstream branch), never prints a value, and check.mjs runs it when the configured npm script is not defined and shows how many lines were scanned. e2e is required once screens are under a browser or visual harness. check.mjs reports the skipped-test count Vitest, Jest, pytest, Mocha, Playwright or node:test printed (skipped ? for test and e2e when none is recognised), reports a node script that is absent, or a Mock2 tool that could not run, as missing, and a rise in skipped tests carries a reason. \u00a7I and the run-ledger rule: a row is identified by its start and surface and never renumbered; min counts active minutes when the clock shows an idle gap over 30 minutes; tool calls are the turn's own count and never copied; a known clock offset goes beside the start time; turn review samples the closing row of each authorized task and rows longer than the largest expected-minutes figure. capacity.md: HANDED OFF (transparent to the breaker walk when it cites its Surfaces line), shared-tree commits, staleness judged per handoff section, a defect this change introduced or put on a path it adds or newly calls is IN SCOPE (one only discovered stays FOLLOW-UP), and FOLLOW-UP lines carry a severity, with security and data-integrity ones marked SECURITY or DATA and named in the handoff the turn they are found. The CPR rule, the production checklist, the TypeScript rule (new: PostgreSQL migrations that keep the application running), the entry block, the Reviewer agent, /mock2-build and the handoff, run-ledger, decisions and work templates carry the same changes. Not a gate. Existing repositories: .mock2/checks.json is project-owned and is not replaced \u2014 set secrets to node .mock2/bin/secrets.mjs, required, and set e2e required where screens are under a harness; until then check.mjs falls back to the scanner whenever the configured npm script is missing. Add ## Surfaces to .github/copilot-instructions.md when more than one surface works the repository. 1.5.0: CPR hosting profile for an application VM on Proxmox, from a production incident on a single-VM deployment and its follow-up. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section L: L.1 storage (SSDs, OS on RAID1, VM storage on a separate RAID10 pool; the pool's sync-write latency is the database's commit floor, enterprise SSDs only when commit speed is a measured problem); L.2 VM baseline (CPU type host, one NUMA socket, memory grows with vCPUs, ballooning off, VirtIO SCSI single with IO thread, discard and SSD emulation, separate 100 GB system and data disks); L.3 resource isolation (cgroup v2 slices with per-vCPU minimums raised as needed, a fit rule recalculated on every resize, parent guarantees equal to the sum of their children's, containers recreated to join their slice, BFQ with systemd's IOWeight mapping, no io.max write caps, swappiness 10, and for blue/green a slot-property swap with a reconciler); L.4 no RAM in production snapshots and time verified against an external reference with a 64 s NTP poll ceiling; L.5 database settings verified on the running container, the data-move procedure and the restart budget; L.6 three-layer pressure diagnosis, the check schedule and a recorded load test after every resize; L.7 development work on the production VM (two concurrent suites budgeted, worker caps, a reviewer reads captured results); L.8 conditional references (caching resolver, local-only mail with scanners). The production checklist gains a Hosting group; the TypeScript rule gains Background jobs and live values (one row per entity per day, one transaction per poll, none across HTTP, notify-then-refetch); the CPR rule gains a Hosting summary. Configuration and reporting only \u2014 no gate. state/work.md records the release. Tooling unchanged. 1.4.0: scope is written once before the first code change, and done is the ID list. New .mock2/capacity.md \u00a713 (Anti-patterns and What this is judged by move to \u00a714 and \u00a715): every Build cycle starts by writing an Authorized scope block at the top of state/work.md \u2014 the outcome in one sentence, the R-### rules or items whose demonstrated acceptance means done, the failure cases that count as part of done (only those the change touches), what is excluded, and any prerequisite found while inspecting with the reason the outcome needs it. Three lines for a small change; never a reason to delay the edit; not rewritten afterwards to match what was built. Every finding during implementation or review gets exactly one label: IN SCOPE (an ID under Done when needs it \u2014 fix it), PREREQUISITE (the outcome cannot be reached without it \u2014 add it with its reason and fix it) or FOLLOW-UP (everything else, including a better design, a case the scope does not promise and a defect elsewhere \u2014 one line under a new Follow-ups section in state/work.md, not worked on this cycle). Only IN SCOPE and PREREQUISITE hold the cycle open; the developer widening the scope is SCOPE CHANGED and recorded in the block. Not a gate. Wired through: the managed entry block's Scope section; /mock2-build step 3 writes the block before deriving items and step 7 labels findings; the Mock2 Build agent; the Mock2 Reviewer reviews against the authorized scope rather than the one it would have chosen and labels every finding with its ID; /mock2-check passes the block and the Follow-ups in the brief and hands only IN SCOPE and PREREQUISITE findings back; the change-record rule carries the label on each review finding and copies every FOLLOW-UP into Open items so nothing is lost when the work file is cleared; the capacity pointer rule summarises it; the repo template's state/work.md gains the block and the Follow-ups section (project-owned \u2014 existing repositories add the section when the rule first applies). Deferred by decision, not forgotten: the rest of the delivery-contract draft of 2026-09-21 (CPR capability-contract format, consequence tiers, status vocabularies, worked example). 1.3.0: the design workflow gains the parts that decide quality, each costing a line of text rather than a step. The page brief now names a REGISTER (operator console \u00b7 review workspace \u00b7 attention queue \u00b7 public/kiosk \u00b7 form-heavy intake \u00b7 editorial) which carries density, type-ramp and motion defaults and selects the inspection list; a screen with no register is not yet briefed. The failure-to-avoid line must be written as checkable statements (\"no more than one badge per row\") rather than a mood. The brief also names the NEIGHBOURING SURFACES the screen sits beside and the SAMPLE DATA the mockup must carry \u2014 a long title, a missing value, one record at the extreme, enough rows for the register's density \u2014 because even, tidy sample data is what makes a competent screen feel plain. FIRST RENDER DEPTH: the first design of a page is never a transcription pass and gets full effort; later revisions on an existing design run at normal depth. Inspection replaces the example list with a FIXED list per register (fixed lists get run, examples get skipped), and adds contrast of each text token against every surface it can land on, not only the page background. Revisions pick the smallest scope that fits and say so \u2014 tweak (re-decide nothing) \u00b7 area \u00b7 screen \u2014 with a RESTYLE EXCEPTION: a revision that respecifies tokens, palette, theme or typeface starts from the new tokens, not from the current stylesheet, which is how an incumbent palette survives an explicit spec; the previous mockup version is linked in the record. Decision records now carry a reason on every rejected direction and a DIAGNOSIS on the last-asked entry (what was wrong, in which dimension), so the next revision does not re-guess. Promotion requires evidence: a screenshot for a project pattern, a before/after pair for a shared-foundation change. The rule states its own cost discipline \u2014 one screen of brief, a fixed short inspection list, appended record lines, findings advisory and never gates. The repo template's state/design/brief.md gains the register, neighbouring surfaces and the checkable failure line. Entry block, prompts and agents unchanged from 1.2.0. 1.2.0: the Mock2 design workflow \u2014 how a screen is decided so the design judgment of one session persists into the next. New rule rules/mock2-design-workflow.md (Stage 1 and any UI change): precedence when sources disagree (explicit request \u2192 page decisions \u2192 project direction and tokens \u2192 shared defaults, accessibility a constraint throughout); a per-project state/design/brief.md and per-page state/design/pages/<page>/brief.md + decisions.md that are kept, never overwritten by another page; a reference library under state/design/references/ indexed by task tags with what-works-and-why, use-when and known weaknesses; the agent prepares the brief and asks at most three consequential questions; ordinary choices resolved before presenting, scaled to the work; render and inspect at desktop and phone, stating what was and was not verified; decision records keep confirmed, provisional, rejected, open, assumptions and last-asked separately \u2014 visual approval never confirms a workflow assumption; revisions change the current accepted design and preserve accepted decisions outside scope; promotion starts at the narrowest justified scope (page \u2192 project pattern \u2192 shared foundation) as Proposals in the change record. The managed entry block gains a UI design and implementation paragraph that loads the rule, the briefs and the matching references before any interface work. /mock2-concept and the Mock2 Concept agent run Stage 1 through the workflow (mockups versioned as mockups/<page>-v<N>.html; the agent, having no shell, says it cannot render rather than implying a visual check). Repo template gains state/design/brief.md and state/design/pages/README.md (project-owned, created once). Tooling unchanged. 1.1.0: CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section K, release mechanics from operation, published from the recorded decisions of one application's release week (the draft previously called Addendum B; no separate addendum document exists). K.1 candidate monitor with hysteresis routes the candidate to review only \u2014 never promotes, rolls back or blocks. K.2 releases are identified by immutable image identity in the Release Registry, with a protected retention set (active, previous, any candidate) that is never pruned. K.3 inactive-slot recovery restores traffic from the retained previous image with no build, and does not reverse a candidate's migration; the release record states whether its migration is additive before promotion. K.4 host-owned fork naming <upstream>-<host>.<m> with the divergence recorded in the decision ledger; configuration and extension points first. K.5 one permission resolver enforced on the server and provider credentials in host services only, binding in J.5. K.6 frontend type-error and lint counts ratchet and visual regression as reporting checks that never sit in front of the test runner. K.7 Express as the cpr-profile runtime, with conventions in rules/mock2-typescript.md. K.8 the --t-*/data-theme decision stays OPEN with option a in force. rules/mock2-typescript.md gains an Express runtime [cpr profile] section: one router per feature, Zod at the route boundary, one error middleware with a stable error shape, authorization through the platform resolver before the handler, /health and /ready, jobs through the job capability, createApp() never listens on import. rules/mock2-production-checklist.md gains a Frontend group (ratchet, visual regression), two platform-boundary items for J.5 and J.3, and three release items for K.1\u2013K.3. state/work.md records the K publication. Tooling unchanged. 1.0.0: CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section J, Base Platform \u2014 what an application inherits and how it must use it. Three standards, three questions: Mock2 (concept to verified implementation), CPR (package, promote, operate, roll back), Base Platform (the capabilities an application inherits), kept as a CPR section until ownership or cadence warrants a split. J defines host as one application deployment's implementation of the Host Contract \u2014 shared Base Platform code never requires a shared runtime, database or release schedule \u2014 and requires each application to record its deployment arrangement with four facts (application, runtime, database/schema, migration owner); the own-guest/own-PostgreSQL arrangement for each existing application and the cpr-starter is marked ASSUMED until confirmed. Every capability is delivered as contract, implementation and starter; a copied starter alone is recorded as having no upgrade path. Capability catalog with eight fixed fields and seven initial entries. Identity and authorization rule: features use the platform contracts, introduce no independent credential store or permission engine, and profiles reference platform identities; one permission resolver, enforced on the server; provider credentials only in host services. Shared-package migrations extend the existing release rules with responsibility split between package author and consuming application; reverting a pin does not reverse its migrations. Distribution requirement is an identified, reproducible release through a supported install and upgrade process \u2014 registry or commit-pinned git dependency; the runtime-dependency upgrade path is marked UNFINISHED. state/work.md records the reuse proof that resolves the ASSUMED and UNFINISHED items. Tooling unchanged. 0.10.4: install.ps1 runs seed-remote.ps1 in the same session as a script block instead of a temp .ps1 file \u2014 Windows' default execution policy (Restricted) refused the file with 'running scripts is disabled on this system', so the remote step at the end of every install failed; iex-style evaluation is not subject to that policy. 0.10.3: install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 read files back as UTF-8 explicitly \u2014 Windows PowerShell's Get-Content reads a BOM-less UTF-8 file in the ANSI code page, so the em dash in the CLAUDE.md marker never matched and every run appended another managed block; the block is now found, refreshed in place, and duplicate copies from earlier runs are removed. The pre-tracking notice and the conflict report give the runnable form of -Force, & ([scriptblock]::Create((irm .../install.ps1))) -Force, since a parameter cannot be passed through irm | iex; -Force backups of a pre-tracking install go under the marker version. A failed code --install-extension shows its last output line instead of only an exit code. 0.10.2: the Windows installer failed every run since checks.json gained an em dash \u2014 Windows PowerShell decoded the JSON response as ISO-8859-1 because nginx served application/json without a charset, so the staged copy hashed differently and install.ps1 refused with 'does not match the release hash'. FetchText in install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 now reads the raw response bytes and decodes UTF-8 itself, independent of headers; nginx additionally sends charset=utf-8 on JSON and JavaScript. 0.10.1: install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 no longer close the PowerShell window. Piped through iex both ran inside the console session and ended every failure and early return with exit, which closed the window and the message with it; the body of each is now one function, failures throw and are printed, early returns return, $ErrorActionPreference is restored, and exit is used only when the script runs as a file. install.ps1 treats an install lock whose owner process is gone as stale whatever its age. Read-Host answers are null-safe when input is redirected. New catalog ~/.mock2/remotes.json: seed-remote.ps1 records every repository it applies to, and its menu begins with Update: host:folder per catalogued repository and Update all; -UpdateAll, -New and -Yes drive it without a menu; a failure in one repository of a batch is reported and the rest continue. install.ps1 reads the same catalog and ends a local install by offering to update the catalogued repositories or set up a new one. Standards text unchanged.",
