arting the run, an inherited MOCK2_RUN_ID and MOCK2_RELAY=off get no finish from it. Tests for each, failing on 1.18.0. Verification rule: every suite a test check wraps runs and is reported, and a test file that fails to load counts every test in it as failed; an intended visual change re-records and inspects its baselines, and a no-sideways-scroll check covers inner scroll regions; compare a candidate's battery with the base's under the same settings, and repair a slow test at the test (one in-process database per file, one real-process scenario per test), never with a longer timeout. Security rule: secrets never go on a command line. CPR summary rule: stored documents stay readable across versions; refreshed tables are upserted, never deleted and refilled. TypeScript rule: schedules catch up; refreshed tables; known traps (extension-loading test engines, production column types in test schemas, a stable auth mock, display utilities over hidden, positioned live regions, client departure by close and writableFinished, page styles over a shared primitive, FOR NO KEY UPDATE on referenced rows, one clock, decoders that never trust a declared size). Capacity: closeout is a listed item. Mobile web: a service-worker update reloads only a page that had a controller; one stacking order above the fixed bottom bar. CPR current \u00a7E amended and \u00a7N.13 added; project names and ids removed from \u00a7O, the mobile-web and CPR summary rules, Relay.md, earlier changelog entries and the site's records, and a test keeps work-item, rule and decision ids out of the published text, the manifest and the records. Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.18.0: New work starts from the release queue's tip, learned from one host where seven candidates were rebased after their final battery in three days because another release landed first. The final battery is a candidate's final-integration run under CPR current \u00a7E; the release queue is the ordered list of candidates whose final battery passed and that have not reached the main branch, each placed by its first pass and appended by the session whose battery passed. A new build starts its branch from the queue's tip, or from the main branch when the queue is empty or none is kept, and an investigation reads that tip and records it; a build does not rebase while it is built and restacks onto the tip ahead of it just before its final battery; queue order is release order. After the final battery a candidate changes without a new battery only by a move its release runner absorbs at its first step (the candidates ahead of it landing, records-only commits): the runner replays only the candidate's own commits, proves the tree differs from the tested commit only in records files, re-runs the outgoing secret scan and any test that reads the records, pushes the result to the candidate branch and records it as the re-pin. Releasing out of queue order is the developer's decision and every queued candidate not containing the released one restacks; a candidate whose parent changed or was withdrawn restacks and revalidates; review compares with the parent's commit. CPR current \u00a7M.7, \u00a7N.8 and \u00a7P.1 amended and \u00a7M.10 added; the verification rule's default order, the CPR summary, the repository entry block, and the build prompt and agent carry the rule. No runner, script or package change; Relay unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.17.0: Checks that take turns, failures proven honestly, and releases guarded by a list, learned from one host where five builds ran full batteries at once. The host decides one check budget for every tree on it (MOCK2_BUDGET_DIR / MOCK2_SHARED_SLOTS or ~/.mock2/budget.json, with an absolute directory, override a repository's budgetDir and sharedSlots); checks marked resource none start as soon as their dependencies pass and never count against maxParallel; reports keep localQueueMs apart from sharedQueueMs and keep pass/fail counts for test checks; a reservation from an earlier boot is freed and a group-writable budget directory is named with its fix; check.mjs --hold [--for <minutes>] pauses other sessions' new heavy checks for a measurement window (their wait does not count against the queue limit; running checks finish first; a hold ends when stopped or when its time runs out); SIGHUP ends a run like SIGINT; a final verification that could not run says so instead of blaming changed inputs; the runner's history identity covers only the git settings that decide history and upstreams, so an editor writing its own branch hints no longer voids a secret scan; --record reads reports kept in subfolders. New bin/battery.mjs runs the battery in a detached scratch clone with copied dependencies checked by the project's battery.verify commands (npm ls --depth=0 checks package.json ranges, not lockfile pins), scans secrets against the worktree's own remote-tracking refs (its origin/HEAD, else its branch's upstream) rather than the clone's copy of local branches, keeps the report and a local output log under .mock2/evidence/<commit>/ and removes the clone on success, failure or signal; a state/relay linked to the shared Relay ledger is telemetry, never a dirty candidate; copy and placeholder paths must resolve inside the clone. doctor shows the check budget in force, names a malformed host budget, and names a secret scan with no comparison branch and its two fixes (the scanner still never guesses a branch). Text: load-dependent failures proven by exact figures, known failures that mask assertions, honest labels for carried-forward results, browser-harness checks (fail fast on unanswered requests, fonts loaded, constant origin across worktree ports), capped test workers, and test traps (test-cost password hashes, fake servers without idle timeouts, signals not sleeps, engines built once per file) (CPR current \u00a7E, \u00a7M.5, \u00a7M.6, verification and TypeScript rules); a fix answering only LOW findings gets a narrow re-review (\u00a7F); the data disk's flush latency measured in the load test, storage moves verified, shared check slots sized from the load test (\u00a7L); host changes reach the main branch before a promotion (\u00a7N.12); records commits checked against an allowed list, never a count, and scanned before the push (\u00a7P.4). The template checks.json marks the secret scan and audit resource none. Version 1.16.0 is reserved for another release and is not used here. 1.15.0: Evidence that is kept, visible and scoped, learned from one application's upgrade-and-release cycle. Known baseline failures are proven once and kept in state/known-failures.md with the inputs they depend on; a change whose run shows the recorded signature, with none of those inputs changed since the proof, cites it instead of re-running the base. The check runner states how it schedules, which checks may reuse evidence and whether Relay check evidence is on; says why when it cannot identify the candidate; names any link leaving the repository, ignored or not, and reuses nothing while one exists; prints a saved run as a Markdown table with --record; and writes Relay's schema-2 check.recorded only when checks.json opts in with relay.checkEvidence (off in the template and in every existing repository), so installing standards no longer makes a repository emit events an older host refuses; MOCK2_RELAY=off makes the check runner record nothing. The secret scan adds --committed for what a push delivers and says how to scope a scan that has no comparison branch. doctor reports runner settings, candidate identity, uncommitted managed files and unignored runtime state; upgrade asks for the upgrade to land as its own commit. Rules and CPR current \u00a7E, \u00a7H, \u00a7I, \u00a7M.9 and \u00a7S amended: proportionate records, interrupted turns keep their ledger row, producers follow their host's Relay reader. The Relay package is unchanged at 1.2.1. 1.14.0: universal integration foundation with separate new-app and incremental existing-app implementation prompts; scoped identity, versioned outcome contracts, per-connection capability evidence, authorized dispatch, preserved working connections and reusable migration/conformance records. Standards upgrades do not silently migrate applications. 1.12.0: Scope-aware verification and explicit evidence reuse, prerequisite scheduling, shared resource budgets, reusable run evidence, outgoing Git history secret coverage, and paired before/after benchmark guidance. Relay 1.2.0 adds versioned Git history observations, bounded resumable setup lookback, execution/reuse/queue telemetry, blue imported activity, and a contained top-tabs/left-work/right-content layout preserving the complete interface. Readers must upgrade before new event types are emitted. 1.11.3: Framework-neutral Relay integration guidance learned from a verified host installation: preserve the full interface and event protocol while reusing host capabilities, distinguish viewer/producer/control readiness, require real authorization and private evidence, keep examples isolated and initial navigation stable, and verify deployment/source recovery with host-specific evidence. Relay runtime remains immutable 1.1.2. 1.11.2: Relay 1.1.2 no longer focuses its heading on mount, preventing the embedded demo from scrolling the homepage to Relay. User-driven view changes retain accessible heading focus with preventScroll. 1.11.1: Relay 1.1.1 fixes execution replay evidence: attachments appear only after their recorded artifact event at the selected history time; current evidence and exact failed-attempt capture remain available. 1.11.0: Restore Relay 1.1.0 design and feature parity: compact metric filters, labeled activity history, selected-task context, visual connections and raster mockups, structured reports, secondary idea-to-outcome history, improved execution evidence, append-only task metadata and authenticated previews. Add complete host-app integration prompt and visual acceptance matrix; preserve binary feature assets through installation and upgrades. 1.10.0: Relay reusable work-visibility package, append-only events, real check instrumentation, authenticated API and resumable SSE, task execution replay, secondary idea lineage, references and acknowledged requests. Host integration remains explicit. 1.9.0: operating lessons from the week of 2026-09-25 to 10-01, when one repository was worked by up to three build sessions and a release runner at once (38 conversations on two surfaces). CPR-v1.1-current.md \u00a7M.4\u2013\u00a7M.8 (where sessions may run concurrently): each conversation owns a worktree and branch for code, mockups, design records and baselines; where the shared records live in the checkout releases run from, that checkout holds only them, edited by targeted insert after a fresh read and committed by the release step, replacing \u00a7M.1's per-worktree handoff and merged-back state; a script that appends never shrinks a shared file; diverged records are reconciled by union; IDs, package versions and harness ports are reserved across trees; a worktree installs to its own lockfile; pause stops and reports, with the row and handoff written on resume; releases go one at a time; a tool with one session slot is held by one session. \u00a7N.7\u2013\u00a7N.11: where a candidate is deployed before promotion, it is deployed before it is merged; the checkout a deploy reads is part of the release, with step-0 checks and verified effects; a runner that hits a guard stops cleanly; a runner never edits application records through a candidate, and scheduled jobs ship a manual trigger; the deploy's migration guard runs as a test. \u00a7N.4: pack once, after review converges. New section P, release runners and the prompts they run: pin and re-pin, define failure narrowly, copy the repository's own commands, write records by explicit path, supersede conditionally. \u00a7E: what a passing check does not show (pre-fix failure of a regression test, attribution against the base by exact figures, re-checks after a rebase, shared styles, late-loading gates and fresh browsers, authorization-migration replay). \u00a7F: every material fix is re-reviewed, and release prompts are reviewed with the code. \u00a7I: exactly one row per turn, completeness tied to a closeout the task includes, host changes in the row with their procedure. The constitution: \u00a70 says a permission denial is not routed around; \u00a74 separates operator-managed integration credentials from application secrets; \u00a77 gives the reviewer any release prompt; \u00a711 gains the confirmed working habits (limiting words, questions answered rather than built, hearing before a merge or deploy when asked, self-contained questions, check before asserting, estimates and progress notes, stop and save, sectioned releases, retention default, live copy and background processes). The design workflow gains reference precedence, two \u00a73 rows (migrating an existing page; capability first, redesign later), redesign depth, the developer's investigate-mockup-stop instruction for area and screen changes, enforced control sizes in mockups, tokens and text going forward, and the interface patterns confirmed in review as shared defaults. The security baseline adds operator-managed credentials (admin interface, encrypted, never .env), credential binding and rotation, published-port exposure, assistant scope, permission migration, host exchange files and typing of copied rows. The TypeScript rule lists known test traps; the run-ledger rule adds completeness, host changes, one row per turn, estimation from history and a daily report format; the CPR rule, production checklist, capacity \u00a77 and \u00a710, the entry block, the review-handoff template and the reviewer carry the new items. The source application's names were removed from the current text (CPR current \u00a7J.2, the \u00a7K status line) and from the 1.0.0 and 1.1.0 entries; the historical CPR documents are unchanged. 1.8.1: phone viewport-sized shells recalculate height on reload/pageshow and visible resume, as well as load, resize, orientation, fullscreen and display-mode changes. The mobile rule supplies a measured-height watcher with validated VisualViewport/window readings, pinch-zoom handling, bounded settling retries and cleanup; shared bottom insets subtract space the measured shell already excludes, clamped to zero, with design padding additive. CPR current \u00a7O.2\u2013O.5 records lifecycle evidence, fallback tradeoffs, height ownership and single accounting of excluded space. The constitution's device principle, managed entry block, design workflow and production checklist carry the lifecycle requirement. Generated repository standards, entry points, template identities and release hashes are rebuilt from canonical sources. One application's fix is the source example; the owner reported success on 2026-10-01, but repeated cold-start/reload/resume steps and other devices are not inferred from that report. Reference fallback values and retry timings remain project decisions supported by evidence. 1.8.0: phones and installed apps become a design principle, so a machine or repository that pulls the standards with the install or seed script carries it in the always-on constitution, not only in a rule loaded for matching files. The constitution gains \u00a713, design principles: every interface is designed within them from its first mockup, beside accessibility at every level of the design workflow's precedence; they never gate building or testing, and a developer's explicit departure from one is followed and recorded. The first is designed for the device and the mode it is used in: a screen used on a phone is designed for its edges and an app with a web app manifest for the installed view as well as the tab, bottom-anchored controls clear the system navigation bar in both whether or not the phone reports an inset, mockups and checks cover both, and a device check includes a cold start; the pattern and the Android fallback stay in rules/mock2-mobile-web.md. \u00a72's Concept stage names the design workflow and \u00a713, and \u00a70 and \u00a710 now write their CPR citations as CPR \u00a7. The entry block's UI paragraph, the design workflow's precedence and page brief, and rules/mock2-mobile-web.md point to \u00a713; the repo template's state/design/brief.md gains a Design principles section that points to \u00a713 (the brief is project-owned; existing repositories may add it, and \u00a713 and the entry block already carry the principle). Existing repositories get the constitution and the entry block through the usual upgrade. 1.7.1: the fallback for Android, corrected the same day (a recorded decision superseding an earlier one; OPEN until a device observation that includes a cold start). 1.7.0 applied the 48 px only when the phone reported an inset under 1 px and less than 48 px of the screen height lay outside the page; the owner confirmed it, and half an hour later, on a later launch, the installed app drew its bottom bar under the navigation buttons again: one of the measured conditions did not hold on that launch (which one was not measured). rules/mock2-mobile-web.md step 4 (Android that reports too little): --nav-fallback is 48 px whenever the app runs installed (display-mode standalone or fullscreen), the user agent is Android and the phone is in portrait, otherwise 0, with no condition on a measured size beyond the orientation and no probe, re-measured on resize and on a standalone or fullscreen display-mode change; the max() at :root still lets a larger reported inset win; the price, a gap of up to 48 px where Android already keeps the installed page above its bar (possibly the usual case) or gesture navigation needs less, is stated in the decision with the revisit trigger (a device still clips, the platform reports the inset, or the developer rejects the gap) and preferred to a control under the system bar; navFallback takes {standalone, android, portrait}. Verify gains an upper bound on every Android case, so an inset added to the fallback fails, a small reported inset, and two screen.height stubs that guard that the removed condition stays removed (the second, the page already above the bar, keeps 48 px as the decided price); the device check includes a cold start, the app closed fully and opened again more than once. CPR-v1.1-current.md \u00a7O.2: the device check covers a cold start; \u00a7O.3: a device heuristic keeps the fewest conditions that hold on every launch, names its price in the decision, and becomes ACTIVE on a device observation that includes a cold start; the CPR rule and the production checklist's Frontend item carry the cold start and the price. Not a gate. A repository that adopted the 1.7.0 fallback drops its reported-inset and screen-height conditions and the probe. 1.7.0: phones and installed web apps, from one defect (one application, 2026-09-30): an installed app on an Android phone with three-button navigation drew its bottom bar under the navigation buttons while every browser check at 375 px, all rendered in the tab, was green; the fix was confirmed on that phone by its owner. New rule rules/mock2-mobile-web.md, installed to ~/.claude/rules and shipped in .mock2/standards/rules/: an installed app can be drawn edge-to-edge behind the system navigation; viewport-fit=cover goes in only together with the inset padding; one inset variable at :root, max(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px), env(safe-area-max-inset-bottom, 0px)), with every env() given its fallback because an unknown name without one voids the declaration, widened by a script-set --nav-fallback; bars pad and floating elements move; the fallback for Android that reports 0 (installed, Android, portrait, reported inset under 1 px, screen.height - innerHeight < 48, then 48 px), read through a hidden probe, kept in one pure unit-tested function and recorded as a decision; the installed view verified in Playwright with a display-mode stub, an Android user agent, a screen.height override and Chromium's Emulation.setSafeAreaInsetsOverride, asserting clearance with lower and upper bounds; display-mode CSS recorded as not verified; a fail-first build that really is the old code. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section O, phones and installed apps: O.1 an installable application is verified in the mode it is used in; O.2 emulation is a substitute measurement and the device check is recorded as done or not verified; O.3 a device heuristic is an OPEN decision until a device observation confirms it. The entry block's UI paragraph loads the rule for phone screens and for any app with a web app manifest; the design workflow inspects bottom-anchored UI with an inset and the installed view; the production checklist's Frontend group and the CPR rule carry \u00a7O. Not a gate. Existing repositories get the rule and the block through the usual upgrade; an app with no bottom-anchored control needs no script. 1.6.0: operating lessons from the week of 2026-09-19, when one repository was worked by four agent surfaces in one working tree. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section M, several agents and surfaces in one repository. M.1: one git worktree per concurrent session, with its costs named (untracked .env, compose names and ports, decision IDs renumbered before merge); where a tree is shared anyway, new files added by name, commits by explicit path with git commit --only -- <paths> and confirmed with git show --stat HEAD, a file both sessions edited committed only from staged hunks or left uncommitted, and the handoff kept one section per session under a stable label. A shared index had put another session's 41 staged files into an unrelated commit. M.2: each surface's capabilities recorded once under ## Surfaces in .github/copilot-instructions.md, and a new turn result HANDED OFF for a turn that finished everything its surface can do and leaves the rest to a named surface by design, citing that line, with a Finish on another surface section in the handoff. BLOCKED stays for unplanned impossibility; 11 of 12 turns on one surface had ended BLOCKED by design, hiding the real blocks. M.3: a check a surface cannot run is routed to one that can, and a measurement on a substitute engine or version says so. Section N, release and data safety from operation. N.1: a migration record states its lock footprint on existing tables; long write locks on tables the application writes are restructured, with constraints validated in a later transaction than the one that adds them. N.2: the release record lists what a candidate can write to shared data, and scheduled writers run only from the promoted release. N.3: a candidate gateway substitutes its own page only when the upstream is unreachable, never for an application error. N.4: a packed package version is never re-packed. N.5: real-provider verification writes go through the audited path, with a read-back and the restore step stated. N.6: supersession names the superseded decision's ID and updates its status in the same edit; an unstated conflict becomes an OPEN decision; a resource-sizing decision names a resize as a revisit trigger. \u00a7E: secrets is a required check. The repository template ships .mock2/bin/secrets.mjs, a scanner that needs nothing installed: it scans lines not yet pushed (against the merge base with the upstream branch), never prints a value, and check.mjs runs it when the configured npm script is not defined and shows how many lines were scanned. e2e is required once screens are under a browser or visual harness. check.mjs reports the skipped-test count Vitest, Jest, pytest, Mocha, Playwright or node:test printed (skipped ? for test and e2e when none is recognised), reports a node script that is absent, or a Mock2 tool that could not run, as missing, and a rise in skipped tests carries a reason. \u00a7I and the run-ledger rule: a row is identified by its start and surface and never renumbered; min counts active minutes when the clock shows an idle gap over 30 minutes; tool calls are the turn's own count and never copied; a known clock offset goes beside the start time; turn review samples the closing row of each authorized task and rows longer than the largest expected-minutes figure. capacity.md: HANDED OFF (transparent to the breaker walk when it cites its Surfaces line), shared-tree commits, staleness judged per handoff section, a defect this change introduced or put on a path it adds or newly calls is IN SCOPE (one only discovered stays FOLLOW-UP), and FOLLOW-UP lines carry a severity, with security and data-integrity ones marked SECURITY or DATA and named in the handoff the turn they are found. The CPR rule, the production checklist, the TypeScript rule (new: PostgreSQL migrations that keep the application running), the entry block, the Reviewer agent, /mock2-build and the handoff, run-ledger, decisions and work templates carry the same changes. Not a gate. Existing repositories: .mock2/checks.json is project-owned and is not replaced \u2014 set secrets to node .mock2/bin/secrets.mjs, required, and set e2e required where screens are under a harness; until then check.mjs falls back to the scanner whenever the configured npm script is missing. Add ## Surfaces to .github/copilot-instructions.md when more than one surface works the repository. 1.5.0: CPR hosting profile for an application VM on Proxmox, from a production incident on a single-VM deployment and its follow-up. CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section L: L.1 storage (SSDs, OS on RAID1, VM storage on a separate RAID10 pool; the pool's sync-write latency is the database's commit floor, enterprise SSDs only when commit speed is a measured problem); L.2 VM baseline (CPU type host, one NUMA socket, memory grows with vCPUs, ballooning off, VirtIO SCSI single with IO thread, discard and SSD emulation, separate 100 GB system and data disks); L.3 resource isolation (cgroup v2 slices with per-vCPU minimums raised as needed, a fit rule recalculated on every resize, parent guarantees equal to the sum of their children's, containers recreated to join their slice, BFQ with systemd's IOWeight mapping, no io.max write caps, swappiness 10, and for blue/green a slot-property swap with a reconciler); L.4 no RAM in production snapshots and time verified against an external reference with a 64 s NTP poll ceiling; L.5 database settings verified on the running container, the data-move procedure and the restart budget; L.6 three-layer pressure diagnosis, the check schedule and a recorded load test after every resize; L.7 development work on the production VM (two concurrent suites budgeted, worker caps, a reviewer reads captured results); L.8 conditional references (caching resolver, local-only mail with scanners). The production checklist gains a Hosting group; the TypeScript rule gains Background jobs and live values (one row per entity per day, one transaction per poll, none across HTTP, notify-then-refetch); the CPR rule gains a Hosting summary. Configuration and reporting only \u2014 no gate. state/work.md records the release. Tooling unchanged. 1.4.0: scope is written once before the first code change, and done is the ID list. New .mock2/capacity.md \u00a713 (Anti-patterns and What this is judged by move to \u00a714 and \u00a715): every Build cycle starts by writing an Authorized scope block at the top of state/work.md \u2014 the outcome in one sentence, the R-### rules or items whose demonstrated acceptance means done, the failure cases that count as part of done (only those the change touches), what is excluded, and any prerequisite found while inspecting with the reason the outcome needs it. Three lines for a small change; never a reason to delay the edit; not rewritten afterwards to match what was built. Every finding during implementation or review gets exactly one label: IN SCOPE (an ID under Done when needs it \u2014 fix it), PREREQUISITE (the outcome cannot be reached without it \u2014 add it with its reason and fix it) or FOLLOW-UP (everything else, including a better design, a case the scope does not promise and a defect elsewhere \u2014 one line under a new Follow-ups section in state/work.md, not worked on this cycle). Only IN SCOPE and PREREQUISITE hold the cycle open; the developer widening the scope is SCOPE CHANGED and recorded in the block. Not a gate. Wired through: the managed entry block's Scope section; /mock2-build step 3 writes the block before deriving items and step 7 labels findings; the Mock2 Build agent; the Mock2 Reviewer reviews against the authorized scope rather than the one it would have chosen and labels every finding with its ID; /mock2-check passes the block and the Follow-ups in the brief and hands only IN SCOPE and PREREQUISITE findings back; the change-record rule carries the label on each review finding and copies every FOLLOW-UP into Open items so nothing is lost when the work file is cleared; the capacity pointer rule summarises it; the repo template's state/work.md gains the block and the Follow-ups section (project-owned \u2014 existing repositories add the section when the rule first applies). Deferred by decision, not forgotten: the rest of the delivery-contract draft of 2026-09-21 (CPR capability-contract format, consequence tiers, status vocabularies, worked example). 1.3.0: the design workflow gains the parts that decide quality, each costing a line of text rather than a step. The page brief now names a REGISTER (operator console \u00b7 review workspace \u00b7 attention queue \u00b7 public/kiosk \u00b7 form-heavy intake \u00b7 editorial) which carries density, type-ramp and motion defaults and selects the inspection list; a screen with no register is not yet briefed. The failure-to-avoid line must be written as checkable statements (\"no more than one badge per row\") rather than a mood. The brief also names the NEIGHBOURING SURFACES the screen sits beside and the SAMPLE DATA the mockup must carry \u2014 a long title, a missing value, one record at the extreme, enough rows for the register's density \u2014 because even, tidy sample data is what makes a competent screen feel plain. FIRST RENDER DEPTH: the first design of a page is never a transcription pass and gets full effort; later revisions on an existing design run at normal depth. Inspection replaces the example list with a FIXED list per register (fixed lists get run, examples get skipped), and adds contrast of each text token against every surface it can land on, not only the page background. Revisions pick the smallest scope that fits and say so \u2014 tweak (re-decide nothing) \u00b7 area \u00b7 screen \u2014 with a RESTYLE EXCEPTION: a revision that respecifies tokens, palette, theme or typeface starts from the new tokens, not from the current stylesheet, which is how an incumbent palette survives an explicit spec; the previous mockup version is linked in the record. Decision records now carry a reason on every rejected direction and a DIAGNOSIS on the last-asked entry (what was wrong, in which dimension), so the next revision does not re-guess. Promotion requires evidence: a screenshot for a project pattern, a before/after pair for a shared-foundation change. The rule states its own cost discipline \u2014 one screen of brief, a fixed short inspection list, appended record lines, findings advisory and never gates. The repo template's state/design/brief.md gains the register, neighbouring surfaces and the checkable failure line. Entry block, prompts and agents unchanged from 1.2.0. 1.2.0: the Mock2 design workflow \u2014 how a screen is decided so the design judgment of one session persists into the next. New rule rules/mock2-design-workflow.md (Stage 1 and any UI change): precedence when sources disagree (explicit request \u2192 page decisions \u2192 project direction and tokens \u2192 shared defaults, accessibility a constraint throughout); a per-project state/design/brief.md and per-page state/design/pages/<page>/brief.md + decisions.md that are kept, never overwritten by another page; a reference library under state/design/references/ indexed by task tags with what-works-and-why, use-when and known weaknesses; the agent prepares the brief and asks at most three consequential questions; ordinary choices resolved before presenting, scaled to the work; render and inspect at desktop and phone, stating what was and was not verified; decision records keep confirmed, provisional, rejected, open, assumptions and last-asked separately \u2014 visual approval never confirms a workflow assumption; revisions change the current accepted design and preserve accepted decisions outside scope; promotion starts at the narrowest justified scope (page \u2192 project pattern \u2192 shared foundation) as Proposals in the change record. The managed entry block gains a UI design and implementation paragraph that loads the rule, the briefs and the matching references before any interface work. /mock2-concept and the Mock2 Concept agent run Stage 1 through the workflow (mockups versioned as mockups/<page>-v<N>.html; the agent, having no shell, says it cannot render rather than implying a visual check). Repo template gains state/design/brief.md and state/design/pages/README.md (project-owned, created once). Tooling unchanged. 1.1.0: CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section K, release mechanics from operation, published from the recorded decisions of one application's release week (the draft previously called Addendum B; no separate addendum document exists). K.1 candidate monitor with hysteresis routes the candidate to review only \u2014 never promotes, rolls back or blocks. K.2 releases are identified by immutable image identity in the Release Registry, with a protected retention set (active, previous, any candidate) that is never pruned. K.3 inactive-slot recovery restores traffic from the retained previous image with no build, and does not reverse a candidate's migration; the release record states whether its migration is additive before promotion. K.4 host-owned fork naming <upstream>-<host>.<m> with the divergence recorded in the decision ledger; configuration and extension points first. K.5 one permission resolver enforced on the server and provider credentials in host services only, binding in J.5. K.6 frontend type-error and lint counts ratchet and visual regression as reporting checks that never sit in front of the test runner. K.7 Express as the cpr-profile runtime, with conventions in rules/mock2-typescript.md. K.8 the --t-*/data-theme decision stays OPEN with option a in force. rules/mock2-typescript.md gains an Express runtime [cpr profile] section: one router per feature, Zod at the route boundary, one error middleware with a stable error shape, authorization through the platform resolver before the handler, /health and /ready, jobs through the job capability, createApp() never listens on import. rules/mock2-production-checklist.md gains a Frontend group (ratchet, visual regression), two platform-boundary items for J.5 and J.3, and three release items for K.1\u2013K.3. state/work.md records the K publication. Tooling unchanged. 1.0.0: CPR-v1.1-current.md gains section J, Base Platform \u2014 what an application inherits and how it must use it. Three standards, three questions: Mock2 (concept to verified implementation), CPR (package, promote, operate, roll back), Base Platform (the capabilities an application inherits), kept as a CPR section until ownership or cadence warrants a split. J defines host as one application deployment's implementation of the Host Contract \u2014 shared Base Platform code never requires a shared runtime, database or release schedule \u2014 and requires each application to record its deployment arrangement with four facts (application, runtime, database/schema, migration owner); the own-guest/own-PostgreSQL arrangement for each existing application and the cpr-starter is marked ASSUMED until confirmed. Every capability is delivered as contract, implementation and starter; a copied starter alone is recorded as having no upgrade path. Capability catalog with eight fixed fields and seven initial entries. Identity and authorization rule: features use the platform contracts, introduce no independent credential store or permission engine, and profiles reference platform identities; one permission resolver, enforced on the server; provider credentials only in host services. Shared-package migrations extend the existing release rules with responsibility split between package author and consuming application; reverting a pin does not reverse its migrations. Distribution requirement is an identified, reproducible release through a supported install and upgrade process \u2014 registry or commit-pinned git dependency; the runtime-dependency upgrade path is marked UNFINISHED. state/work.md records the reuse proof that resolves the ASSUMED and UNFINISHED items. Tooling unchanged. 0.10.4: install.ps1 runs seed-remote.ps1 in the same session as a script block instead of a temp .ps1 file \u2014 Windows' default execution policy (Restricted) refused the file with 'running scripts is disabled on this system', so the remote step at the end of every install failed; iex-style evaluation is not subject to that policy. 0.10.3: install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 read files back as UTF-8 explicitly \u2014 Windows PowerShell's Get-Content reads a BOM-less UTF-8 file in the ANSI code page, so the em dash in the CLAUDE.md marker never matched and every run appended another managed block; the block is now found, refreshed in place, and duplicate copies from earlier runs are removed. The pre-tracking notice and the conflict report give the runnable form of -Force, & ([scriptblock]::Create((irm .../install.ps1))) -Force, since a parameter cannot be passed through irm | iex; -Force backups of a pre-tracking install go under the marker version. A failed code --install-extension shows its last output line instead of only an exit code. 0.10.2: the Windows installer failed every run since checks.json gained an em dash \u2014 Windows PowerShell decoded the JSON response as ISO-8859-1 because nginx served application/json without a charset, so the staged copy hashed differently and install.ps1 refused with 'does not match the release hash'. FetchText in install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 now reads the raw response bytes and decodes UTF-8 itself, independent of headers; nginx additionally sends charset=utf-8 on JSON and JavaScript. 0.10.1: install.ps1 and seed-remote.ps1 no longer close the PowerShell window. Piped through iex both ran inside the console session and ended every failure and early return with exit, which closed the window and the message with it; the body of each is now one function, failures throw and are printed, early returns return, $ErrorActionPreference is restored, and exit is used only when the script runs as a file. install.ps1 treats an install lock whose owner process is gone as stale whatever its age. Read-Host answers are null-safe when input is redirected. New catalog ~/.mock2/remotes.json: seed-remote.ps1 records every repository it applies to, and its menu begins with Update: host:folder per catalogued repository and Update all; -UpdateAll, -New and -Yes drive it without a menu; a failure in one repository of a batch is reported and the rest continue. install.ps1 reads the same catalog and ends a local install by offering to update the catalogued repositories or set up a new one. Standards text unchanged.",
